<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">White Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The White Revolution was a programme of reforms launched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-reza-pahlavi">Mohammad Reza Shah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and approved by referendum in January 1963. It included land reform, the sale of state factories, profit-sharing for workers, a literacy corps and votes for women. It weakened landlords and clergy, and opposition to it brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> to prominence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-revolution.jpg" alt="White Revolution" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mrplandreform1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The White Revolution, also called the Shah and People Revolution, was a series of reforms to modernise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, announced by the Shah in January 1963 and extended over some sixteen years to nineteen elements. The first six were introduced on 9 January 1963 and put to a national referendum on 26 January. They were land reform, the nationalisation of forests and pastures, the sale of state-owned factories, profit-sharing for industrial workers, the vote for women and a Literacy Corps, in which high school graduates could serve their military service as village teachers.</p><p>Under the land reform, landlords were compensated with shares in privatised state factories, and the land was sold to peasants at 30 percent below market value on a loan payable over 25 years. Later elements added a Health Corps, a Reconstruction and Development Corps, village Houses of Equity with five elected elders, the nationalisation of water resources, free and compulsory education, social security and measures against corruption. The Shah hoped through land reform to ally himself with the peasantry and sever its ties with the aristocracy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the referendum 5,598,711 people voted for the reforms and 4,115 against, although the Shah's opponents boycotted it. The landlords objected to selling land at a lower price, and the Shi'a clergy objected to the loss of their traditional powers in education and family law, to the vote for women and to the break-up of lands held under charitable trusts, a considerable part of their income.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> became the most open critic. In a speech at the Feyziyeh School in June 1963 he attacked the Shah as a person, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a> he called him a wretched miserable man. Two days later, on 5 June (15 Khordad), he was arrested, and three days of rioting left several hundred dead, though Khomeini claimed the army had slaughtered no less than 15,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Supporters point to new roads, factories, dams and schools. Enrolment in elementary schools rose from 1,640,000 to 4,080,000, the literacy rate from 26 to 42 percent, and women gained the right to stand for office and to serve as lawyers and later judges. Critics note that only about half of the rural population received land, often too little to live on, and that a new group of commercial farmers emerged while many villagers migrated to cities. The reforms made Khomeini a figure around whom opponents could rally, and the failures of land reform, the lack of democratic change and the hostility of the clergy and landed elites contributed to the Shah's downfall and the Revolution of 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “White Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1963 Mohammad Reza Shah’s <b>White Revolution</b> gave Iranian women the vote, and during the revolution of 1979 many of the rights the women’s movement had gained, such as the Family Protection Law, were systematically abolished.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The shah's <b>White Revolution</b> of 1963 brought land reform and women's suffrage, while the SAVAK secret police suppressed opponents.</blockquote><a href="/state/pahlavi-iran">Pahlavi Iran</a></li></ul></section>
